before:
ToArray makes a separate array. Changing that copy does not change the source; choose a view or a copy deliberately.

ToArray() takes no arguments and returns a new array containing the view’s elements. This separate example changes only that copy:
```csharp
using System;
int[] original = { 4, 5 };
int[] copy = original.AsSpan(0, 1).ToArray();
copy[0] = 9;
Console.WriteLine($"{original[0]} {copy[0]}");
```
Output: 4 9.

Complete local C# example; the app does not execute it:
```csharp
using System;
int[] values = [10, 20, 30];
Span<int> slice = values.AsSpan(1, 2);
slice[0] = 99;
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", values));
```

after:
Follow the view back to the array slot it shares.

```csharp
using System;
int[] values = [10, 20, 30];
Span<int> slice = values.AsSpan(1, 2);
slice[0] = 99;
Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", values));
```
